-- title:
--   Rational places generate Pic⁰ over a base point
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra. A place of $F$ over $K$ is, in this setting, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; its degree is the $K$-dimension of its residue field, and a divisor is a finitely supported function from places to $\mathbb{Z}$, the degree homomorphism sending a divisor to $\sum_v D(v)\,\deg v$, with `Divisor.degZero` its kernel and $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$ the quotient of `Divisor.degZero` by the subgroup of principal divisors lying in it. Assume every place of $F$ over $K$ has degree $1$, fix a place $V_0$, and let $x$ be an arbitrary element of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$. The conclusion is that $x$ belongs to the additive subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$ generated by the set of those classes of the form `Pic0.mk` applied to the divisor $[V]-[V_0]$, i.e. $\mathrm{Finsupp.single}\,V\,1-\mathrm{Finsupp.single}\,V_0\,1$ together with a proof that it has degree zero, as $V$ runs over all places of $F$ over $K$.
--
--   This is the elementary statement that, when all places are rational, the degree-zero divisor class group is generated by the Abel–Jacobi type classes $[V]-[V_0]$ based at a fixed place. It serves to reduce assertions about arbitrary degree-zero classes to assertions about differences of two points, and is used for instance in the study of the action of semilinear automorphisms and inertia on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and in the reduction-theoretic statements about points of modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_mem_closure_mk_single_sub_single.lean

